WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.9 · Financial Management
Audit, examination and financial governance

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Agree who oversees the money before you have a board, and set a first reserves target.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.652
Financial governance is how you oversee money: approving the budget, reviewing the accounts and setting reserves. Depending on your form, size and country, independent people may also need to check your accounts.

4
00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:27.652
Who checks your accounts

5
00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:38.981
Founder review: The founders now, and a board later, read, question and approve the accounts. For example: Both founders go through the annual accounts line by line before signing them.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:49.941
Independent examination: A qualified independent person reviews the books and accounts; lighter than an audit. For example: A small registered charity has its accounts examined by a qualified accountant.

7
00:00:49.941 --> 00:01:01.270
Statutory audit: An auditor tests the records and gives a formal opinion; usually later, as you grow. For example: Once income passes the national threshold, the cooperative appoints an auditor.

8
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:14.541
Funder audit: A check of how one grant was spent, set out in the grant agreement. For example: A funder asks for an audit of its start-up grant to the youth club.

9
00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:15.541
Why oversight matters early

10
00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:21.215
1. Audit thresholds bring costs and deadlines you need to plan for.

11
00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:27.630
2. Independent eyes on the numbers protect the founders as well as the money.

12
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:33.563
3. A reserves target is your first defence against shocks.

13
00:01:33.963 --> 00:01:34.563
What you will set

14
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:40.237
1. Who approves the budget, reviews the accounts and approves major commitments.

15
00:01:40.237 --> 00:01:46.281
2. Which audit or examination will apply, and when, confirmed for your country.

16
00:01:46.281 --> 00:01:53.696
3. A reserves policy: a target, the reason for it and a first step.

17
00:01:54.096 --> 00:01:54.696
What to avoid

18
00:01:54.696 --> 00:01:60.000
Assuming no checks apply because you are small or new.

19
00:01:60.000 --> 00:02:05.304
Founders reviewing only their own work, with no independent look.

20
00:02:05.304 --> 00:02:11.607
Setting no reserves target because you have no reserves yet.

21
00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:14.007
You write your external scrutiny, oversight and first reserves policy.

22
00:02:14.007 --> 00:02:18.007
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:19.507 --> 00:02:22.607
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
